ar_1_1a_math_i_calculus_p_test_fe8ed10f_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why must behaviour near infinity and behaviour near zero be treated separately in the $p$-test?","options":["Because the conditions are reversed: $p<1$ at infinity and $p>1$ near zero","Because the integral at infinity converges for every $p>0$, as $t^{-p}\\to0$","Because the conditions differ: $p>1$ at infinity, but $p<1$ near zero","Because $p=1$ gives convergence near zero but divergence at infinity"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"388295831d242b10d77d6a39","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_p_test_fe8ed10f_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What determines convergence for the improper integrals $\\int_{a}^{\\infty} t^{-p}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\int_{0}^{a} t^{-p}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$?","options":["Only the numerical value of a.","Only the interval type and the value of p.","Only whether p is an integer.","Only whether the integrand is written using x or t."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bc95fa777f631a1215ac66a5","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_p_test_fe8ed10f_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Use the $p$-test to classify ∫_3^∞ 1/$\\sqrt{t}$ dt.","options":["It converges, because $p = 1/2$ < 1.","It converges, because 3 is positive.","It diverges, because square roots are not allowed in the $p$-test.","It diverges, because $p = 1/2$ is not greater than 1."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"77fd5c50ce7c8898a4ba2eb4","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_p_test_fe8ed10f_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Use the $p$-test to classify ∫_0^2 1/$\\sqrt{t}$ dt.","options":["It diverges, because $p = 1/2$ < 1.","It converges, because $p = 1/2$ < 1.","It converges, because $p = 1/2$ > 1.","It diverges, because the upper bound is 2."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ac84ce4dd442060d7f3e77ef","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_p_test_fe8ed10f_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement correctly describes the boundary case $p = 1$ in the $p$-test?","options":["It converges at infinity but diverges near zero.","It diverges both at infinity and near zero.","It diverges at infinity but converges near zero.","It converges both at infinity and near zero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f5a24fde0390550bdbc20b26","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_p_test_fe8ed10f_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $a>0$ and $p\\in\\mathbb{R}$. A student claims that convergence of $\\int_a^\\infty t^{-p}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ implies convergence of $\\int_0^a t^{-p}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. Why is this implication invalid?","options":["Convergence at infinity would require the same inequality as convergence near zero.","The near-zero integral has no singularity whenever $a$ is positive.","The value of $a$ changes the exponent $p$.","The $p$-test gives opposite strict inequalities for the two endpoint behaviours."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4da06edb9b098f8b2f19a4ef","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_p_test_fe8ed10f_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For which value of p does $\\int_{1}^{\\infty} t^{-p}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ converge while $\\int_{0}^{1} t^{-p}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ diverges?","options":["$p = 2$","$p = 1/2$","$p = 0$","$p = -1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9d625b8c2120296321da3989","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_p_test_fe8ed10f_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Which pair of conclusions is correct for $p = 1/2$?","options":["$\\int_{1}^{\\infty} t^{-1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ converges and $\\int_{0}^{1} t^{-1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ converges.","$\\int_{1}^{\\infty} t^{-1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ converges and $\\int_{0}^{1} t^{-1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ diverges.","$\\int_{1}^{\\infty} t^{-1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ diverges and $\\int_{0}^{1} t^{-1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ converges.","$\\int_{1}^{\\infty} t^{-1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ diverges and $\\int_{0}^{1} t^{-1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ diverges."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4cec7e2376b7809e30e80fad","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"p_test_new_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $\\int_a^{+\\infty}t^{-p}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ with $a \\gt 0$, what is the convergence condition?","options":["$p \\gt 1$","$p \\lt 1$","$p=1$","$p \\lt 0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4c3ddf42e37383f83e248356","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"p_test_new_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $\\int_0^a t^{-p}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ with $a \\gt 0$, what is the convergence condition?","options":["$p \\lt 1$","$p \\gt 1$","$p=1$","$